Subject: [PATCH v9 00/10] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's mail hub
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:04:57 +0000

This builds the hub that "[PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec
the list's mail hub" [1] describes, on b/dev. It turns the relay jail
into the list's home in steps, one service at a time. Each step
leaves a jail that works and does what its README.org says.

How mail moves, end to end, once the series is applied:

  client --SMTP--> OpenSMTPD --LMTP--> Dovecot --> INBOX
  Dovecot, every folder <--IMAP--> mbsync <--IMAPS--> Fastmail

New mail lands in INBOX, whether it is posted to lab-mail or arrives
at Fastmail. mbsync syncs every folder of the account both ways, and
makes on lab-mail any folder Pat makes on Fastmail. Only the clients
name folders: INBOX, Archive, Later, Next, and quarantine. There is
no Sieve and no Fastmail rule. Dovecot is the only program that
writes the maildir. Every reader may read every folder, and only Pat
and mbsync may change anything.

- Patch 1 corrects the spec on what Dovecot does to a reader's write.
  A STORE or EXPUNGE answers OK and changes nothing. Only append,
  copy, and move are refused. Acceptance step 7 now expects that.
- Patch 2 narrows the spec's header check to what OpenSMTPD does on
  its own: it refuses a header line that is neither a field nor a
  continuation, and on loopback adds a missing Message-ID and Date.
- Patch 3 makes Dovecot the only writer of the list. mbsync still
  fetches one way, but into Dovecot over IMAP as the login mbsync,
  not into the maildir. This is the one step that needs the maildir
  and mbsync's state emptied first.
- Patch 4 turns the fetch into a two-way sync of lists and Archive.
- Patch 5 adds Pat's login, so he can archive and delete on lab-mail.
- Patch 6 swaps smtprelay for OpenSMTPD, which hands posts to Dovecot
  over LMTP, into INBOX. smtprelay, jq, and the mailcred group go
  with it.
- Patch 7 documents the one-time import of the jj maildir.
- Patch 8 adds lab@lists.p7x.dev and freebsd-ports@lists.p7x.dev to
  the addresses OpenSMTPD delivers to the list.
- Patch 9 puts INBOX in the maildir and syncs every folder, so a post
  reaches Fastmail and every reader on the next sync. mbsync gets the
  ACL's create right, for folders made on Fastmail. lists goes, with
  the Fastmail rule that filled it, and so does the spec's moderation
  queue. The import lands in INBOX.
- Patch 10 specs the client side of Next, Later, and quarantine. The
  folder is a message's only state, and clients derive their tags
  from it. Pat's notmuch syncs flags, and the agent's does not.

lists is gone from lab-mail and from every client's folder list. Any
mail still in it on Fastmail stays there, and mbsync would carry it
like any other folder until you delete the folder on Fastmail.

For every step I checked that each libexec script passes sh -n.
mbsync 1.5.1 parses the mbsyncrc of patch 9. Dovecot, smtpd, and the
jail build are not installed here, so no step was run on a jail.

Changes in v9:
- Patch 9 syncs every folder (Patterns *) with Create Near, instead
  of the explicit INBOX, Archive, and lists, since only the clients
  limit folders (Pat). mbsync's ACL entry gains the create right
  again, and Dovecot no longer creates lists. lists is dropped from
  the config, README.org, and spec.org.
- Patch 10 is new. It replaces "[PATCH] jails/instances/lab-mail:
  Sync the Next and Later folders" [2], posted on its own on top of
  v8. With every folder synced, lab-mail no longer names Next and
  Later, so what is left is the client spec, now with quarantine too.
- Patches 1 to 8 are unchanged.
